WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.740
It doesn’t feel quite real to say this&nbsp;
but… mission accomplished. I did it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.940
I actually made it through. My extraction is&nbsp;
currently en route, and once they’re here, I’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.800
be heading back to the SCP Foundation. It doesn’t&nbsp;
feel like a hundred days since this all started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.620
if anything it feels like a lot longer. But&nbsp;
it’s done now, the mission is finally over – and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.480
now the real work begins. I’ll be taken in for&nbsp;
debriefing when I arrive, so I’d better get my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.460
story straight before that transport gets here.
Here’s how I Survived 100 Days Infiltrating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.580
the Chaos Insurgency.
It started with a message;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:38.280
a direct request passed down straight to me from&nbsp;
the O5 Council themselves. I was shocked initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.080
to receive any communication from them; the&nbsp;
Council were hardly hands-on bosses, preferring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.600
to co-ordinate the Foundation from a station far,&nbsp;
far above mine. I was part of a Mobile Task Force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.520
used to being out in the field, so when I got the&nbsp;
message – saying the Council wanted to talk to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.180
me – I wasn’t quite sure what to make of it.
I went through various different states of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.100
reaction in my head, first being honored that&nbsp;
they had singled me out. That then turned into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:07.020
worry – had I done something wrong, or was this&nbsp;
some kind of dismissal from the Foundation? Was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.160
it a performance review? When it finally came&nbsp;
time for me to stand before the O5 Council,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.820
the last thing that I was expecting&nbsp;
to hear was that they had selected me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:20.760
specially for a dangerous undercover assignment.
Of course, I had heard of the Chaos Insurgency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.560
before. Although I was yet to actually&nbsp;
encounter them in the field, the MTFs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.820
were all instructed to keep up to date on who the&nbsp;
Foundation’s enemies were. As a Group of Interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.740
it wasn’t hard to see why they were such a&nbsp;
threat. A heavily armed, clandestine organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.440
with goals of weaponizing SCPs and achieving&nbsp;
world domination: they sounded like lunatics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.640
but dangerous lunatics all the same.
That was the first step in my mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.620
know your enemy. I was instructed to&nbsp;
familiarise myself with the Chaos Insurgency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.560
to understand them inside and out. While the MTF&nbsp;
agent in me wanted to just get boots to ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:02.040
and start the real work, this operation needed a&nbsp;
different approach. And so, I hit the books; or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.980
to put it more accurately, I began scouring the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation’s database for any and all intel on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.320
the Insurgency. All I could do was sit and read.
Within forty-eight hours of being given my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.400
assignment, I felt like I knew everything&nbsp;
about the Chaos Insurgency back to front,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.960
sideways, upside down, and pretty&nbsp;
much any and every way possible. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:27.900
imagine how disheartened I felt when I reported my&nbsp;
research to the O5 Council, only to be told that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.120
I hadn’t done enough. Hardly the best feeling,&nbsp;
I won’t deny it stung to hear that my day of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.340
research wasn’t adequate prep for my assignment.
But the Council did have an important point:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:44.160
this was a covert, deep-cover infiltration into&nbsp;
one of the most dangerous groups that opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.800
the Foundation. Purely memorizing all manner of&nbsp;
facts and figures about the Insurgency, learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.180
of all their tactical capabilities, reading up on&nbsp;
the various rumors about how they were founded;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.940
all that was just knowledge. If I wanted to pass&nbsp;
myself off as a part of the Chaos Insurgency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:05.220
and avoid detection, knowing wasn’t&nbsp;
enough. I had to think like one of them.&nbsp;

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.780
The timeline of the mission began to accelerate&nbsp;
rapidly. I would have to train myself to believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:15.000
the Insurgency’s core beliefs as I went along;&nbsp;
events weren’t going to wait for me to ne ready.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.920
The Council informed me that they were aware of&nbsp;
a double agent working for the Chaos Insurgency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.680
from within the SCP Foundation itself.
Obviously, I had heard rumors that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.820
were operatives embedded within the ranks of&nbsp;
the Foundation, but what was more surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.620
was that the O5 Council seemed to already know&nbsp;
who these agents were. I had always assumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.120
that these Insurgents kept their identities&nbsp;
a secret, so to hearing that a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.640
researcher – Doctor Parsons – was a known Chaos&nbsp;
Insurgency conspirator and still had a job at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.880
the Foundation was both startling and worrying.
But he provided me a way in, and I assumed that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.760
why the O5 Council allowed the Insurgency’s&nbsp;
double agents to remain as ‘undetected’ as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.500
thought they were. It meant they could be used as&nbsp;
tools for the Foundation’s benefit to get at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:07.500
Insurgency – they were double agents, after all.
Doctor Parsons was due to be at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.940
Foundation site I was stationed at for only&nbsp;
a few days. The upside of working directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:16.020
on the orders of the Council meant that&nbsp;
they had the administerial reach to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.280
all the necessary strings to get this&nbsp;
duplicitous double agent in the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:26.340
place for me to intercept him, but not for long.
The window of opportunity was only a small one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.620
I had a short time to get close enough to Parsons&nbsp;
and get him to put me in contact with his friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:37.380
at the Chaos Insurgency. Of course, I had to&nbsp;
be tactful about it. If I came on too strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:42.720
there was a strong chance the double agent would&nbsp;
easily detect that something was off and that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:48.600
cover had been blown. So, the plan was simple:&nbsp;
strike up a conversation with the spy, in a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:54.000
that wouldn’t arouse suspicion and would compel&nbsp;
him to assist my joining the Chaos Insurgency.&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.780
Actually, ‘simple’ might not&nbsp;
have been the right word.&nbsp;

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:03.120
The day of my confrontation with Parsons&nbsp;
I was uncontrollably nervous. I could feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:08.580
myself shaking and silently hoped that it wasn’t&nbsp;
noticeable to anyone looking at me. The mess hall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:13.500
was decided as the best place I could drum&nbsp;
up some kind of conversation with him. As I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.480
picked up my food tray, I spotted the doctor&nbsp;
taking a seat in a far corner. Cautiously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.040
I approached and asked if I could join him.
In the back of my head, I kept reminding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.900
myself I had to play it casual. If I went in&nbsp;
immediately with claiming I felt the Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.400
should be run differently, then Parsons would&nbsp;
smell the deception, and the whole mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.620
would come apart. So instead, I started small.&nbsp;
We drummed up a conversation about the quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.540
of the food the Foundation catering team had&nbsp;
given us – or rather, the lack of quality.&nbsp;

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:46.320
Then we chatted about containment&nbsp;
procedures, and various anomalies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.000
that we had each encountered in our different&nbsp;
jobs. Lunch was wrapping up, we’d both finished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.560
our food and I knew my window was about to&nbsp;
close. And so, I threw in to conversation:&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.220
“You ever get sick of the way they do things?”
The entire next day, I was on edge, worried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:06.000
that I’d thrown my assignment and alerted&nbsp;
Parsons that the Foundation was aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.160
his ties to the Insurgency. I didn’t leave&nbsp;
the Task Force barracks, confining myself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.380
my quarters. If I left, there was always a&nbsp;
slim chance of running into Doctor Parsons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:20.580
or that the Council would call me in to&nbsp;
reprimand how I handled first contact.&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:26.280
To my surprise, my paranoid thoughts were broken&nbsp;
by a direct message from Parsons. He mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.260
being intrigued by some of our conversation the&nbsp;
previous day, and being disappointed I hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:37.140
been in the mess hall this lunchtime to continue&nbsp;
discussing certain aspects. It felt weirdly nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:42.060
being friendly, only to then remember the man&nbsp;
was a double agent for the Chaos Insurgency.&nbsp;

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:47.760
His message went on to suggest we should meet&nbsp;
in private in his office. And that’s when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:53.280
tension really hit me. Either he wanted me dead&nbsp;
with no witnesses, or he had bought my line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.800
about disagreeing with the Foundation’s methods.
I arrived at Parsons’ office at the agreed time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.420
only to be welcomed warmly by the&nbsp;
doctor. It was instantly disarming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:09.600
and intentionally so. I tried not to fall for&nbsp;
it, just in case he knew what I was really up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:15.420
to. But at the same time, I couldn’t let it show.
Doctor Parsons seemed to pick up our discussion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:19.860
from the other day, immediately talking&nbsp;
about how the SCP Foundation would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:24.720
better off putting anomalies to use rather&nbsp;
than keeping them contained. I was surprised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.000
how brazen he was being, but then again,&nbsp;
the Council knew he was a mole, maybe they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.400
had bugged his office, and he hadn’t realized.
Nodding along, pretending to agree with what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:40.860
doctor was saying, the conversation quickly&nbsp;
progressed. I told Parsons I felt the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:46.320
and it was frustrating to me as an MTF agent,&nbsp;
seeing so many co-workers die in containment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:52.500
breaches against SCPs. That was the moment he&nbsp;
mentioned he had ‘some friends I should meet.’&nbsp;

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:57.420
The following night, they came for me while I&nbsp;
slept in the barracks. I had no idea who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:02.580
drugged me, although I suspected it was Doctor&nbsp;
Parsons. He wasn’t there when I woke up though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.440
in fact, I couldn’t really tell who was&nbsp;
at first. The combination of the bag over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.760
my face and my head still reeling from&nbsp;
whatever had knocked me out saw to that.&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.340
Wherever I was, I didn’t get to see it.&nbsp;
There were figures near me, at least two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:23.520
that much I could make out. Especially when the&nbsp;
interrogation started. They were Chaos Insurgency;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:29.280
their line of questioning told me that. Between&nbsp;
punches and tasing, their questions ranged from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.900
what I was really up to, to whether the Foundation&nbsp;
had sent me to infiltrate their organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:38.580
They’d anticipated the Council would use their&nbsp;
own tactics against them, but every time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:44.520
no matter how much pain they inflicted, I&nbsp;
denied it. I wasn’t a mole, I told them over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:51.120
and over again: I believed what they believed.
After over a week of torture, the bag came off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.460
and I was greeted by the sight of my captors in&nbsp;
Chaos Insurgency tactical gear. They informed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.600
me that I had passed the initial phase&nbsp;
of their recruitment process, and after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.460
suffering so much, that should have come as&nbsp;
a relief. My mission to infiltrate them was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:11.100
progressing, but their interrogation had&nbsp;
left me exhausted and in intense pain.&nbsp;

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:16.380
The pair of Insurgents made it very clear that&nbsp;
I wasn’t one of them though, not yet. In order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:20.640
to fully join their ranks, they wanted to&nbsp;
send me on an operation with one of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:26.340
armed detachments. If I complied with orders, and&nbsp;
made a deliberate act against the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:32.040
that would tell them that I was ready to become&nbsp;
part of the Chaos Insurgency. If I refused,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:36.600
or the mission was deemed to be a failure,&nbsp;
it would mean an on-the-spot execution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:41.820
Hiding my reluctance, knowing I wouldn’t be&nbsp;
able to forewarn the Foundation, I agreed.&nbsp;

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:46.800
I spent the next several days with a new group&nbsp;
of Insurgency operatives. None of them gave me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.140
their names, nor any actionable intel I could&nbsp;
pass on to the Council. I just had to refer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:57.360
to them by their various Gamma and Beta class&nbsp;
numbers, their ranks within the Chaos Insurgency.&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:03.720
The mission was an extraction; as part of their&nbsp;
efforts to utilize anomalies, the Insurgency would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:10.230
often launch violent raids on the Foundation to&nbsp;
capture SCPs. Their target this time was SCP-2800,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:17.100
the Cactusman. He was mostly harmless save for his&nbsp;
spines, but the group’s leaders, Delta Command,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:23.400
wanted to force SCP-2800 to join their ranks.
I had to treat it like any other operation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:29.100
my MTF, trying to force myself to forget that I&nbsp;
was on the wrong side as we broke into the Site&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:34.680
containing SCP-2800. He didn’t want to leave,&nbsp;
but I hoped that the Foundation would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:39.660
launch a counter mission to rescue him. All&nbsp;
that mattered for now was making the Insurgency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:46.440
see that I could be helpful, and useful to them.
Sure enough, the Foundation did recover SCP-2800.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:52.020
And by the time they did, the Chaos Insurgency&nbsp;
had already welcomed me into their ranks. I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:57.600
proven myself and that I shared their beliefs in&nbsp;
utilizing the anomalous to realize their goals.&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.880
During the mission, I had made a point of showing&nbsp;
my experience and skills as an MTF operative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.860
hoping that would impress them. It seemed to&nbsp;
have done the trick, as I was made a member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:12.000
of Beta Class, still under the command of&nbsp;
Gamma and the Delta Command above them.&nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.640
But it was a start, at least, not as low&nbsp;
in the chain as the Alpha Class were. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.440
were Insurgency recruits with no experience&nbsp;
dealing with anomalies, and who only joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.180
to escape their miserable lives. Delta&nbsp;
Command considered Alpha Class expendable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:31.500
as if they were little more than just cannon&nbsp;
fodder. I couldn’t help but feel bad for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:36.180
especially when I realized that the Foundation&nbsp;
treated their D Class exactly the same.&nbsp;

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:42.180
Even the MTFs were mostly foot soldiers to them…
Now established within the Insurgency’s ranks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:46.800
I spent the next two weeks finding out everything&nbsp;
I could about their operations and reporting back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:51.660
to the O5 Council through a secure, encrypted&nbsp;
channel. Part of my infiltration mission was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:57.240
to see if I could locate the Engine, a mysterious&nbsp;
anomalous artifact that had apparently influenced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:02.940
the earliest members – now Delta Command – to&nbsp;
form the Chaos Insurgency in the first place.&nbsp;

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:07.620
There was no sign of the Engine anywhere in&nbsp;
the compound I was stationed at. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:12.840
its location was likely a closely guarded&nbsp;
secret. I did, however, encounter one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:19.980
anomalies within the Insurgency’s possession. It&nbsp;
was SCP-1316, what appeared to be an ordinary and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:26.460
harmless kitten named Lucy. But in actuality,&nbsp;
SCP-1316 was an intelligent and sophisticated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:32.100
listening device used to spy on the Foundation&nbsp;
and report back to the Insurgency; I made sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:36.720
to warn the Council the moment I found out,&nbsp;
letting them know it wasn’t just double agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:41.820
being used to report on Foundation activity.
Just as things were starting to form a regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:47.820
pattern, things seemed to start going south. A&nbsp;
few of my ‘fellow’ Insurgents seemed to mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:53.160
things in conversation that made me start to feel&nbsp;
paranoid. They weren’t directly referencing me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.840
but they talked openly about what they’d do if&nbsp;
they found out the Foundation had tried to spy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:03.120
on the Insurgency; and none of it was pleasant.
I spent the rest of the day wracked with worry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.740
There was no way they could have known I was&nbsp;
communicating with the O5 Council, or that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:12.840
the only reason I was there had been to gather&nbsp;
intelligence on the Chaos Insurgency. It could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:17.640
have just been what people talked about here,&nbsp;
although I couldn’t shake my concern that maybe my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:23.580
cover had been blown, that this was their subtle&nbsp;
way of telling me the Insurgency was onto me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:28.980
The worrying left me no choice, and I raised my&nbsp;
concerns with the Council the first chance I got.&nbsp;

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:34.080
Unfortunately, the response I received the&nbsp;
following day did little to subdue my worries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:40.440
The O5 Council contacted me, reminding me of the&nbsp;
importance of my mission. I was to infiltrate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:46.020
report back to them, find the Engine or at least&nbsp;
its location. There was an extraction planned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:51.720
arriving in secret in a month’s time. That&nbsp;
was my time limit, and a warning came with it:&nbsp;

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:56.280
“If you can’t complete this assignment, then&nbsp;
don’t bother returning to the Foundation,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.280 --> 00:14:02.700
the Council told me. “If you are unsuccessful&nbsp;
in carrying out your objectives, it will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:07.680
taken as an act of sedition. We will consider&nbsp;
you an active member of the Chaos Insurgency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:13.320
and thus a threat to the Foundation.”
I was furious, not to mention scared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:17.160
out of my mind. The Council was willing to&nbsp;
turn their backs on me if I couldn’t deliver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:21.480
and if the Insurgency found out they had&nbsp;
a mole in their ranks, then it sounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.500
like they were ready to kill me on the spot.
The Insurgency were gearing up for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:30.720
mission, this time a more aggressive attack on the&nbsp;
Foundation as opposed to the stealthier extraction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:36.300
that I’d previously been on. Surprisingly, that&nbsp;
put me more at ease; sure, it’d be dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:42.480
but this was what I was good at. In the&nbsp;
field intensity, combating anomalies. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:47.580
could use this as an opportunity to regain some&nbsp;
footing, remind the Chaos Insurgency what made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:53.220
me useful to them… or so I thought.
The mission was to capture SCP-682,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:58.680
and fit it with a piece of technology&nbsp;
the Insurgency had tested on SCP-2394,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:04.800
a form of sapient tree frog. Their plan was the&nbsp;
device would enable them to control the Hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:10.620
Destroy Reptile, meaning the Insurgency would have&nbsp;
an unstoppable, adaptable killing machine in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:17.280
arsenal. The attack on the Foundation site was a&nbsp;
bloodbath, MTFs beset the Insurgents on all sides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:23.280
And as for 682… well, it wasn’t exactly known&nbsp;
for coming quietly without putting up a fight.&nbsp;

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:28.440
Returning from the field, after only a few days,&nbsp;
I was confronted by a higher-up, a member of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:32.820
Gamma Class that oversaw the Insurgency’s&nbsp;
operations. He told me that there had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:37.620
numerous reports from other operatives, that they&nbsp;
hadn’t seen me killing or even injuring Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:44.040
personnel during the failed extraction of SCP-682.
It was true, I was still loyal to the Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:49.500
after all. The people working there were still&nbsp;
my co-workers, even if I was pretending to be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:54.900
Insurgent. I’d deliberately missed shots, still&nbsp;
using my weapon in the firefights with MTFs, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.900 --> 00:16:00.300
without causing them harm. I’d hoped nobody in the&nbsp;
Insurgency would notice, but it seemed they had.&nbsp;

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:05.400
I brushed off the accusation, made some&nbsp;
excuse about needing to go for an eye exam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:10.680
and get contact lenses. But under the surface&nbsp;
were relentless shudders of panic. In hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:14.700
the Gamma officer had told me this so&nbsp;
the Insurgency could see how I reacted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:19.320
but at the time, I didn’t realize…&nbsp;
and that played right into their hand.&nbsp;

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:24.780
The next day I was caught while going to send&nbsp;
an urgent SOS message to the O5 Council. Before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:30.060
I could reach the place I stashed by encrypted&nbsp;
comm device, a group of Chaos Insurgency Beta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:34.320
class operatives rushed me. I was bagged&nbsp;
again, pulled away kicking and screaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:38.040
and unable to call for help…
Over the next few days, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:43.380
like I’d been sent back in time to the start of my&nbsp;
infiltration. As the Insurgency tortured me again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:48.780
I refused to give in, despite how much I wanted&nbsp;
to just to make the pain stop. But even then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:52.800
it didn’t matter. Their knowledge of the&nbsp;
Foundation was greater than I realized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:58.260
and the Insurgents were easily able to crack the&nbsp;
encryption of my comm unit. With that broken, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:03.360
had access to all the messages I’d been sending&nbsp;
to the O5 Council; and realizing who had sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.700
me made them change their interrogation methods.
The Insurgents revealed something I hadn’t heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:13.980
before, something the Council had apparently&nbsp;
kept a secret. The Chaos Insurgency had once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:19.440
been a part of the SCP Foundation. I refused&nbsp;
to believe it at first, but with all I’d seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:25.560
as an MTF agent, even my undercover mission, it&nbsp;
started to make sense. An off-the-record black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:31.380
ops unit working for the O5 Council to circumvent&nbsp;
the Ethics Committee. They’d grown disillusioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:37.200
with how the Foundation handled its anomalies&nbsp;
and broken away to form… the Chaos Insurgency.&nbsp;

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:41.280
When I accepted that to be true, the&nbsp;
Insurgents brought me to what I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:47.460
been sent for. They took me to the Engine.
Now I’m awaiting my extraction transport. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:51.240
showing me the device that caused their earliest&nbsp;
founders to splinter off from the Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:56.040
the Chaos Insurgency took me several miles&nbsp;
from their compound, to the co-ordinates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.040 --> 00:18:00.900
O5 Council had told me to be at. The Foundation&nbsp;
think they’re getting their agent back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:07.080
and with me, plenty of intel on their enemies.
But the Engine showed me the truth. Showed me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:12.720
the lies and fallacies of the Foundation,&nbsp;
and especially of the O5 Council. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:17.580
think they’re getting back their agent…&nbsp;
but I serve the Chaos Insurgency now.&nbsp;

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:24.000
Now go check out “SCP CHAOS INSURGENCY EXPLAINED”&nbsp;
and “I SURVIVED 100 DAYS HIDING FROM THE SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:28.500
FOUNDATION... HERE’S WHAT HAPPENED” for&nbsp;
more mind-blowing centennial challenges!

